/*
osc.lcb - xTalk Builder (LCB) binding library for Open Sound Control, via the
          osc_shim C layer over tinyosc. The oscxt member of the xTalk suite
          (born as the OSC third of ShowControl for OpenXTalk).

It targets OpenXTalk / the xTalk language family and is compatible with
LiveCode 9.6.3+. Once this extension is loaded, every public handler below is
callable directly from xTalk script. Script has no [...] list literal, so a
script builds the FLAT argument list by assignment (examples/osc-helpers'
scAddArg does it for you):

    local tArgs, tData
    put "f" into tArgs[1]
    put 0.5 into tArgs[2]
    put oscBuildMessage("/1/fader1", tArgs) into tData
    write tData to socket "127.0.0.1:9000"   -- a socket opened first (see helpers)

and LCB code writes the same list as ["f", 0.5].

This extension does ENCODING/DECODING only. Transport is the script's own
LiveCode UDP socket (the engine owns the socket and the run loop), so nothing
here ever spawns a thread or calls back into script -- inbound OSC arrives in a
normal `socketReceived` handler (docs/getting-started.md, "Receiving").

Conventions (shared across the suite):
  * Handles are integers (0 = invalid); the C shim validates them, so a stale
    handle is a harmless no-op. Script never sees a handle -- every public
    handler brackets create...use...free internally.
  * Byte buffers (OSC datagrams, blobs) are LiveCode `Data`. They cross the FFI
    as a (Pointer, length) pair -- see the MARSHALLING note below.
  * Short strings (address, type tag, errors) cross as ZStringUTF8.
  * 64-bit integers / timetags have no foreign type on the 9.6.3 floor, so they
    cross as decimal STRINGS.
  * Nothing here throws for bad INPUT: a refusal returns an empty result and
    oscLastError() says why. A throw means the native library is missing or is
    not the ABI this binding was built for.

The foreign handlers bind to the shared library "oscxt" (built by
CMakeLists.txt and bundled inside the extension under
code/<arch>-<platform>/oscxt.{so,dll,dylib}). The exported C ABI symbols keep
the stable "osc_" prefix.

  --- MARSHALLING (ShowControl's Phase 0 spike, RESOLVED; CLAUDE.md "Gotchas") ---
  A `Data` does NOT auto-bridge to a foreign `Pointer` (the LCB Language
  Reference: "No automatic bridging from Data or String to Pointer exists" -- a
  Data marshals as an MCDataRef). Passing one fails at call time with "Value is
  not of correct type for passing as argument". The headless lc-run suite
  (tests/lcb/) reproduced exactly that against the old binding. So, matching the
  proven xtalk-suite (TorrentXT) idiom:
    * INPUT buffers (C reads them) pass MCDataGetBytePtr(theData) -- the
      read-only pointer to the Data's own bytes -- plus `the number of bytes in`.
    * OUTPUT buffers (C fills them) are a raw block from the engine <builtin>
      MCMemoryAllocate (one scratch block, grown on demand and reused); the
      written bytes are copied back out with MCDataCreateWithBytes.
  Only dataPtr / ensureScratch / scratchToData touch this; the C ABI is unchanged.
*/

/**
Builds and reads Open Sound Control (OSC 1.0) messages and bundles, the
protocol that control surfaces, lighting desks, media servers and music
software use to talk over a network.

This library only turns values into OSC bytes and OSC bytes back into
values. The network is the stack's own: send the bytes with a datagram
socket (`open datagram socket`, then `write`), and receive them with
`accept datagram connections ... with message`. The osc-helpers script
(examples/osc-helpers.livecodescript) does both for you and routes each
incoming message to an `scOscMessage` handler of your own.

To send, make a message with <oscBuildMessage> (several at once with
<oscBuildBundle>). To receive, read the bytes with <oscParse>, which returns
an array, and test addresses with <oscMatch>. Nothing here throws for bad
input. A refusal returns an empty result, and <oscLastError> says why.

Built on tinyosc by Martin Roth.

Tags: Networking, OSC, Show control, Library
*/
library org.openxtalk.library.osc

use com.livecode.foreign

metadata title is "OSC for OpenXTalk"
metadata author is "Seth Morrow Software"
metadata version is "0.1.0"

-- =====================================================================
-- Foreign bindings to the shim (private)
-- =====================================================================
-- NO binding here RETURNS a C string. The engine wraps a returned ZString* in a
-- foreign value whose finalizer free()s the pointer (libscript module-foreign.cpp
-- __cbuffer_finalize), so returning a static buffer crashes the engine and
-- returning a pointer into a parsed model double-frees it -- the headless suite
-- crashed on oscLastError() alone until this was changed. Every string comes back
-- through the shim's caller-buffer entry points (bytes-written / -needed) into
-- the scratch block, then is decoded as UTF-8 (see shimText).
private foreign handler _abi_version() returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_abi_version!cdecl"

-- building
private foreign handler _build_new(in pAddr as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_new!cdecl"
-- A value that arrives as a String crosses as text and is parsed in C (the *_str
-- entry points); LCB will not coerce a decimal String to its Number type. A
-- value that arrives as a Number crosses exactly: f and d as the C float/double
-- it is (osc_build_add_float/_double, in the C ABI since ABI 1), i h t and s as
-- the exact decimal text addArg writes for it (see numberArgText).
private foreign handler _build_add_float(in pH as CInt, in pV as CFloat) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_float!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_double(in pH as CInt, in pV as CDouble) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_double!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_string(in pH as CInt, in pV as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_string!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_blob(in pH as CInt, in pData as optional Pointer, in pLen as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_blob!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_true(in pH as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_true!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_false(in pH as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_false!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_nil(in pH as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_nil!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_impulse(in pH as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_impulse!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_int64_str(in pH as CInt, in pDec as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_int64_str!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_timetag_str(in pH as CInt, in pDec as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_timetag_str!cdecl"
-- Numeric build args cross as decimal STRINGS (ABI 2): the engine hands a script
-- number to a foreign `any` as a string, and LCB will not coerce a decimal string
-- to its Number type, so we pass the string and parse it in C (as for int64).
private foreign handler _build_add_int32_str(in pH as CInt, in pDec as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_int32_str!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_float_str(in pH as CInt, in pDec as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_float_str!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_add_double_str(in pH as CInt, in pDec as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_add_double_str!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_finish(in pH as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_finish!cdecl"
private foreign handler _build_free(in pH as CInt) returns nothing binds to "c:oscxt>osc_build_free!cdecl"

-- bundles
private foreign handler _bundle_new_str(in pDec as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_bundle_new_str!cdecl"
private foreign handler _bundle_add_message(in pH as CInt, in pMsg as optional Pointer, in pLen as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_bundle_add_message!cdecl"
private foreign handler _bundle_finish(in pH as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_bundle_finish!cdecl"
private foreign handler _bundle_free(in pH as CInt) returns nothing binds to "c:oscxt>osc_bundle_free!cdecl"

-- parsing
private foreign handler _osc_parse(in pData as optional Pointer, in pLen as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_parse!cdecl"
private foreign handler _is_bundle(in pH as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_is_bundle!cdecl"
private foreign handler _bundle_timetag_str(in pH as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_bundle_timetag_str!cdecl"
private foreign handler _bundle_count(in pH as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_bundle_count!cdecl"
private foreign handler _bundle_message(in pH as CInt, in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_bundle_message!cdecl"
private foreign handler _osc_address(in pH as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_address!cdecl"
private foreign handler _osc_typetag(in pH as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_typetag!cdecl"
private foreign handler _arg_count(in pH as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_arg_count!cdecl"
private foreign handler _arg_type(in pH as CInt, in pIndex as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_arg_type!cdecl"
private foreign handler _arg_int32(in pH as CInt, in pIndex as CInt, out pOk as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_arg_int32!cdecl"
private foreign handler _arg_float(in pH as CInt, in pIndex as CInt, out pOk as CInt) returns CFloat binds to "c:oscxt>osc_arg_float!cdecl"
private foreign handler _arg_double(in pH as CInt, in pIndex as CInt, out pOk as CInt) returns CDouble binds to "c:oscxt>osc_arg_double!cdecl"
private foreign handler _arg_string(in pH as CInt, in pIndex as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_arg_string!cdecl"
private foreign handler _arg_int64_str(in pH as CInt, in pIndex as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_arg_int64_str!cdecl"
private foreign handler _arg_blob(in pH as CInt, in pIndex as CInt, in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_arg_blob!cdecl"
private foreign handler _parse_free(in pH as CInt) returns nothing binds to "c:oscxt>osc_parse_free!cdecl"

-- matching + diagnostics
private foreign handler _osc_match(in pPattern as ZStringUTF8, in pAddress as ZStringUTF8) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_match!cdecl"
private foreign handler _last_error(in pOut as optional Pointer, in pCap as CInt) returns CInt binds to "c:oscxt>osc_last_error!cdecl"

-- POSIX loader assist (Linux): preload the shim by absolute path so the
-- engine's later bare-name "c:oscxt>" bind resolves to the resident copy.
-- dlerror() returns libc-owned text, so it comes back as a raw Pointer and is
-- COPIED with MCStringCreateWithCString -- never as a ZString the engine would free.
private foreign handler _dl_open(in pPath as ZStringNative, in pFlags as CInt) returns optional Pointer binds to "c:>dlopen!cdecl"
private foreign handler _dl_error() returns optional Pointer binds to "c:>dlerror!cdecl"

-- Engine <builtin> helpers for the byte-buffer FFI (libfoundation/foundation.h).
-- A `<builtin>` handler resolves by its NAME matching the engine symbol, so these
-- -- unlike every other private foreign handler here -- carry NO leading `_`.
-- Declared before any helper uses them (OXT resolves identifiers lexically).
--   * MCDataGetBytePtr: read-only pointer to a Data's own bytes (INPUT buffers).
--   * MCMemoryAllocate/Deallocate: a raw writable block (OUTPUT buffers).
--   * MCDataCreateWithBytes: copy C-written bytes back into a Data.
--     A Data's byte count is uindex_t == uint32 (CUInt); the size is UIntSize.
private foreign handler MCDataGetBytePtr(in pData as Data) returns Pointer binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCMemoryAllocate(in pSize as UIntSize, out rBlock as Pointer) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCMemoryDeallocate(in pBlock as Pointer) returns nothing binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCDataCreateWithBytes(in pBytes as Pointer, in pCount as CUInt, out rData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
--   * MCStringDecode: bytes -> String (kMCStringEncodingUTF8 == 4).
--   * MCStringCreateWithCString: COPY a NUL-terminated native C string we do not own.
private foreign handler MCStringDecode(in pData as Data, in pEncoding as CInt, in pIsExternalRep as CBool, out rString as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCStringCreateWithCString(in pCString as Pointer, out rString as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"

-- =====================================================================
-- Byte-buffer marshalling (the only code that touches raw pointers)
-- =====================================================================
-- One reusable OUTPUT block, grown on demand. The capacity (0 until the first
-- allocation) doubles as the "is it allocated?" flag, so an unset Pointer is
-- never read. It persists for the process: there is no library-unload hook to
-- free it at, and reuse keeps the build/parse hot path allocation-free.
variable sScratchPtr as Pointer
variable sScratchCap as Integer

-- A refusal decided HERE, before anything reaches the shim: an unknown type
-- code, a value of the wrong kind, a NUL character. The shim clears its own
-- error at the start of every build, so these used to leave oscLastError()
-- EMPTY beside an empty result. Each public build or parse handler clears it on
-- entry, and oscLastError() reports it ahead of the shim's.
variable sBindingError as String

-- The reason the last oscLoadNativeLib failed, read from dlerror() at the
-- moment of the failure and kept. Read there, not later: glibc frees the text
-- dlerror() returned at the NEXT dl* call in the process, and the engine makes
-- such calls itself, binding each foreign handler on its first call through
-- dlsym (engine note 6.19). ShowControl's oscLoadNativeLibError read dlerror()
-- when it was called: its first answer after a failure was empty, and its
-- second the freed text (OBSERVED under lc-run, 2026-11-21).
variable sLoadError as String

private handler ensureScratch(in pNeed as Integer)
   variable tOk as Boolean
   if sScratchCap >= pNeed then
      return
   end if
   if sScratchCap > 0 then
      unsafe
         MCMemoryDeallocate(sScratchPtr)
      end unsafe
      put 0 into sScratchCap
   end if
   put false into tOk
   unsafe
      put MCMemoryAllocate(pNeed, sScratchPtr) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      throw "osc: failed to allocate a scratch buffer"
   end if
   put pNeed into sScratchCap
end handler

-- Copy the first pLen bytes the shim wrote into the scratch block out as Data.
private handler scratchToData(in pLen as Integer) returns Data
   variable tData as Data
   variable tOk as Boolean
   put the empty data into tData
   if pLen <= 0 then
      return tData
   end if
   unsafe
      put MCDataCreateWithBytes(sScratchPtr, pLen, tData) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      return the empty data
   end if
   return tData
end handler

-- The read-only pointer to an INPUT Data's bytes. Callers pass it with `the
-- number of bytes in` that Data and keep the Data alive across the call (it is
-- held by their own variable/parameter, so it is).
private handler dataPtr(in pData as Data) returns Pointer
   variable tPtr as Pointer
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetBytePtr(pData) into tPtr
   end unsafe
   return tPtr
end handler

-- Decode the first pLen bytes of the scratch block as UTF-8 text.
private handler scratchText(in pLen as Integer) returns String
   variable tData as Data
   variable tStr as String
   variable tOk as Boolean
   put the empty string into tStr
   if pLen <= 0 then
      return tStr
   end if
   put scratchToData(pLen) into tData
   unsafe
      put MCStringDecode(tData, 4, false, tStr) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      return the empty string
   end if
   return tStr
end handler

-- Which caller-buffer string getter shimText calls (literal, declared before use).
private constant kTxtAddress is 1
private constant kTxtTypetag is 2
private constant kTxtArgString is 3
private constant kTxtArgInt64 is 4
private constant kTxtTimetag is 5
private constant kTxtError is 6

-- Fetch one string from the shim through its caller-buffer contract: the call
-- returns bytes written (>= 0), or -needed when the buffer is too small (grow
-- once and retry). A negative that already fits (osc_arg_string's -1 for "not a
-- string") or a bad handle yields empty -- never a throw, and never a C string
-- the engine would free.
private handler shimText(in pWhich as Integer, in pH as Integer, in pIndex as Integer) returns String
   variable tRc as Integer
   variable tTry as Integer
   variable tNeed as Integer
   put 256 into tNeed
   repeat with tTry from 1 up to 2
      ensureScratch(tNeed)
      unsafe
         if pWhich is kTxtAddress then
            put _osc_address(pH, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tRc
         else if pWhich is kTxtTypetag then
            put _osc_typetag(pH, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tRc
         else if pWhich is kTxtArgString then
            put _arg_string(pH, pIndex, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tRc
         else if pWhich is kTxtArgInt64 then
            put _arg_int64_str(pH, pIndex, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tRc
         else if pWhich is kTxtTimetag then
            put _bundle_timetag_str(pH, sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tRc
         else
            put _last_error(sScratchPtr, sScratchCap) into tRc
         end if
      end unsafe
      if tRc >= 0 then
         return scratchText(tRc)
      end if
      if -tRc <= sScratchCap then
         return the empty string
      end if
      put -tRc into tNeed
   end repeat
   return the empty string
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- The library
-- =====================================================================
/**
Summary: Returns the ABI number of the native library that is loaded.

Returns: The ABI number; this binding needs 2.

Example:
put oscVersion() into field "status"

Description: Every other call checks this number first and throws when it is
not 2, which means the native library and this binding come from different
releases.
*/
public handler oscVersion() returns Integer
   variable tR as Integer
   unsafe
      put _abi_version() into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR
end handler

private handler checkABI()
   variable tV as Integer
   unsafe
      put _abi_version() into tV
   end unsafe
   if tV is not 2 then
      throw "osc: incompatible native library (binding needs ABI 2) - rebuild/repackage the osc shim"
   end if
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Building messages and bundles
-- =====================================================================
-- Does this text hold a NUL? A NUL cannot cross as a ZString: the engine
-- THROWS "cannot export char U+0000 in nul-terminated string buffer" into
-- script at the call, where every other bad value is a quiet empty result.
private handler hasNul(in pText as String) returns Boolean
   return pText contains (the char with code 0)
end handler

-- The exact decimal digits of a whole Number inside +-2^64, with a "-" when it
-- is negative. Below 2^53 mod and divide are exact on a double; a larger value
-- is halved down to there (exact: it is even) and the halvings are multiplied
-- back in decimal, digit by digit.
private handler wholeNumberText(in pValue as Number) returns String
   variable tV as Number
   variable tNeg as Boolean
   variable tShift as Integer
   variable tDigits as List
   variable tD as Number
   variable tN as Number
   variable tCarry as Number
   variable tK as Integer
   variable tText as String
   put pValue < 0 into tNeg
   if tNeg then
      put -pValue into tV
   else
      put pValue into tV
   end if
   put 0 into tShift
   repeat while tV >= 9007199254740992.0
      divide tV by 2
      add 1 to tShift
   end repeat
   put [] into tDigits
   repeat forever
      put tV mod 10 into tD
      push tD onto tDigits
      put (tV - tD) / 10 into tV
      if tV < 1 then
         exit repeat
      end if
   end repeat
   repeat tShift times
      put 0 into tCarry
      repeat with tK from 1 up to the number of elements in tDigits
         put tDigits[tK] * 2 + tCarry into tN
         put tN mod 10 into tDigits[tK]
         put (tN - (tN mod 10)) / 10 into tCarry
      end repeat
      if tCarry > 0 then
         push tCarry onto tDigits
      end if
   end repeat
   put the empty string into tText
   repeat with tK from 1 up to the number of elements in tDigits
      put (tDigits[tK] formatted as string) & tText into tText
   end repeat
   if tNeg then
      put "-" & tText into tText
   end if
   return tText
end handler

-- The text the shim parses for a Number given to an i, h, t or s argument.
-- Why not `formatted as string`: LCB writes a Number held as a real with "%g",
-- six significant digits (libscript module-arithmetic.cpp,
-- MCArithmeticExecFormatNumberAsString), and a Number is held as a real
-- whenever script or LCB COMPUTED it: `put 1000 * 1234 into tArgs[2]` sent the
-- integer 1 (from "1.234e+06"), `the milliseconds` as h sent 1, and 0.0000001
-- as i sent 1. i h t take the whole part, as the text path reads "2.7" (2) and
-- "-2.7" (-2); outside +-2^64 they get text the shim saturates on, and NaN reads
-- as 0. s writes a whole Number in full and anything else as LCB always has.
private handler numberArgText(in pValue as Number, in pType as String) returns String
   if pType is "s" then
      if pValue mod 1 is 0 and pValue < 9007199254740992.0 and pValue > -9007199254740992.0 then
         return wholeNumberText(pValue)
      end if
      return pValue formatted as string
   end if
   if pValue < 18446744073709551616.0 and pValue > -18446744073709551616.0 then
      return wholeNumberText(pValue - (pValue mod 1))
   end if
   if pValue > 0 then
      return "99999999999999999999"
   end if
   if pValue < 0 then
      return "-99999999999999999999"
   end if
   return "0"
end handler

-- Append one [type, value] pair to an open builder handle. A refusal returns
-- false with sBindingError saying why; the caller frees the builder.
--   T F N I   no value (anything given is ignored)
--   b         Data, sent byte for byte
--   i f d s h t   a Number (sent exactly, see numberArgText) or a String
--             (parsed in C: i h t as whole decimal text, f d as decimal text
--             with a "." in any locale, s verbatim as UTF-8)
private handler addArg(in pH as Integer, in pType as String, in pValue as optional any) returns Boolean
   variable tStr as String
   variable tData as Data
   variable tNum as Number
   if pType is "T" or pType is "F" or pType is "N" or pType is "I" then
      unsafe
         if pType is "T" then
            _build_add_true(pH)
         else if pType is "F" then
            _build_add_false(pH)
         else if pType is "N" then
            _build_add_nil(pH)
         else
            _build_add_impulse(pH)
         end if
      end unsafe
      return true
   end if
   if pType is "b" then
      -- A blob must be Data (a String has no defined byte encoding here).
      if not (pValue is a data) then
         put "oscBuildMessage: a b (blob) value must be binary data" into sBindingError
         return false
      end if
      put pValue into tData
      unsafe
         if the number of bytes in tData > 0 then
            _build_add_blob(pH, dataPtr(tData), the number of bytes in tData)
         else
            _build_add_blob(pH, nothing, 0)
         end if
      end unsafe
      return true
   end if
   if pType is not "i" and pType is not "f" and pType is not "d" and pType is not "s" and pType is not "h" and pType is not "t" then
      put "oscBuildMessage: unknown type code '" & pType & "'" into sBindingError
      return false
   end if
   if pValue is nothing then
      put "oscBuildMessage: type '" & pType & "' has no value" into sBindingError
      return false
   end if
   if pValue is a number then
      put pValue into tNum
      if pType is "f" then
         unsafe
            _build_add_float(pH, tNum)
         end unsafe
         return true
      end if
      if pType is "d" then
         unsafe
            _build_add_double(pH, tNum)
         end unsafe
         return true
      end if
      put numberArgText(tNum, pType) into tStr
   else if pValue is a string then
      put pValue into tStr
      if hasNul(tStr) then
         put "oscBuildMessage: a string value cannot contain a NUL character" into sBindingError
         return false
      end if
   else
      put "oscBuildMessage: a '" & pType & "' value must be a number or a string" into sBindingError
      return false
   end if
   unsafe
      if pType is "i" then
         _build_add_int32_str(pH, tStr)
      else if pType is "f" then
         _build_add_float_str(pH, tStr)
      else if pType is "d" then
         _build_add_double_str(pH, tStr)
      else if pType is "s" then
         _build_add_string(pH, tStr)
      else if pType is "h" then
         _build_add_int64_str(pH, tStr)
      else
         _build_add_timetag_str(pH, tStr)
      end if
   end unsafe
   return true
end handler

-- Serialize an open builder/bundle handle into Data using the two-call
-- size-then-fill protocol (pFinish is "message" or "bundle").
private handler finishToData(in pH as Integer, in pKind as String) returns Data
   variable tNeed as Integer
   variable tCap as Integer
   variable tWrote as Integer
   unsafe
      if pKind is "bundle" then
         put _bundle_finish(pH, nothing, 0) into tNeed
      else
         put _build_finish(pH, nothing, 0) into tNeed
      end if
   end unsafe
   if tNeed >= 0 then
      return the empty data          -- 0 = error (oscLastError set) or empty
   end if
   put -tNeed into tCap
   ensureScratch(tCap)
   unsafe
      if pKind is "bundle" then
         put _bundle_finish(pH, sScratchPtr, tCap) into tWrote
      else
         put _build_finish(pH, sScratchPtr, tCap) into tWrote
      end if
   end unsafe
   return scratchToData(tWrote)
end handler

-- oscBuildMessage(pAddress, pArgs) -> Data
--   pArgs is a FLAT list alternating type, value, type, value, ... -- e.g. the
--   elements "i", 3, "f", 0.5, "s", "hi". The scAddArg helper (in
--   examples/osc-helpers.livecodescript) builds exactly this for you.
--   Types: i f d s h t (a Number or a String; h and t are 64-bit, so give a
--   value above 2^53 as a decimal String) b (Data) T F N I (no value -- pass
--   empty, or leave a final one dangling).
--   A value of the wrong kind (an Array, a Boolean, a String blob), an unknown
--   type code, a NUL character in a string, or a trailing value type with no
--   value builds NOTHING (empty Data), and oscLastError() says why.
--   Why flat and NOT a list of [type, value] pairs: a nested LiveCode Script array
--   does not survive the Script->LCB boundary as a list-of-lists -- the inner pair
--   arrives as an Array, not a List -- so we flatten to scalars, which cross cleanly.
/**
Summary: Builds one OSC message, ready to send.

pAddress: The OSC address, such as "/1/fader1". It starts with "/".

pArgs: The arguments as ONE flat list: a type code, then its value, then the
next type code, and so on. Type codes: "i" (32-bit integer), "f" (32-bit
float), "d" (64-bit float), "s" (text), "h" (64-bit integer), "t" (timetag),
"b" (binary data), and "T", "F", "N", "I" (true, false, nil and impulse,
which take no value: give empty).

Returns: The message as binary data, or empty when it cannot be built.

Example:
local tArgs, tData
put "f" into tArgs[1]
put 0.5 into tArgs[2]
put oscBuildMessage("/1/fader1", tArgs) into tData
write tData to socket "127.0.0.1:9000"

Description: Values for i, f, d, s, h and t may be numbers or text. A
number is sent exactly; text is read as a number with a "." for the decimal
point, whatever the computer's language. Give an h or t value above 2^53 as
text, since a script number cannot hold it exactly. An i value outside the
32-bit range is clamped to it.

A value of the wrong kind, an unknown type code, a type with no value, or a
NUL character in the address or a text value builds nothing: the result is
empty and <oscLastError> says why.

The osc-helpers script's scAddArg command builds the flat list for you.
*/
public handler oscBuildMessage(in pAddress as String, in pArgs as List) returns Data
   variable tH as Integer
   variable tElem as any
   variable tType as any
   variable tHaveType as Boolean
   variable tArgOk as Boolean
   variable tResult as Data
   checkABI()
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   if hasNul(pAddress) then
      put "oscBuildMessage: the address cannot contain a NUL character" into sBindingError
      return the empty data
   end if
   unsafe
      put _build_new(pAddress) into tH
   end unsafe
   if tH is 0 then
      return the empty data
   end if
   -- Walk the flat list two elements at a time: odd element = type code, even = value.
   -- tType is carried as 'any' and bound to addArg's String parameter once it
   -- is known to BE a String (a Number there threw at the call).
   put false into tHaveType
   repeat for each element tElem in pArgs
      if not tHaveType then
         if not (tElem is a string) then
            put "oscBuildMessage: a type code must be a string such as 'i'" into sBindingError
            unsafe
               _build_free(tH)
            end unsafe
            return the empty data
         end if
         put tElem into tType
         put true into tHaveType
      else
         put addArg(tH, tType, tElem) into tArgOk
         put false into tHaveType
         if not tArgOk then
            unsafe
               _build_free(tH)
            end unsafe
            return the empty data        -- refused: fail quietly, build nothing (sBindingError says why)
         end if
      end if
   end repeat
   -- A trailing type with no value: natural for the no-value types (T F N I),
   -- so accept it; for a value type it is a malformed list, so fail the build
   -- (addArg rejects `nothing` as a value) instead of silently dropping the arg.
   if tHaveType then
      put addArg(tH, tType, nothing) into tArgOk
      if not tArgOk then
         unsafe
            _build_free(tH)
         end unsafe
         return the empty data
      end if
   end if
   put finishToData(tH, "message") into tResult
   unsafe
      _build_free(tH)
   end unsafe
   return tResult
end handler

-- oscBuildBundle(pTimetag, pMessages) -> Data
--   pTimetag is a decimal NTP timetag String ("1" == immediately).
--   pMessages is a list of Data, each a finished OSC message (from oscBuildMessage).
/**
Summary: Builds an OSC bundle: several messages sent as one datagram.

pTimetag: When the receiver should act, as an NTP timetag in decimal text.
"1" means immediately.

pMessages: A list of messages, each built by <oscBuildMessage> (or another
bundle).

Returns: The bundle as binary data, or empty when it cannot be built.

Example:
local tMsgs, tBundle
put oscBuildMessage("/a", empty) into tMsgs[1]
put oscBuildMessage("/b", empty) into tMsgs[2]
put oscBuildBundle("1", tMsgs) into tBundle

Description: An element that is empty or is not binary data makes the whole
bundle fail: the result is empty and <oscLastError> says why.
*/
public handler oscBuildBundle(in pTimetag as String, in pMessages as List) returns Data
   variable tH as Integer
   variable tElem as optional any
   variable tMsg as Data
   variable tResult as Data
   checkABI()
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   if hasNul(pTimetag) then
      put "oscBuildBundle: the timetag cannot contain a NUL character" into sBindingError
      return the empty data
   end if
   unsafe
      put _bundle_new_str(pTimetag) into tH
   end unsafe
   if tH is 0 then
      return the empty data
   end if
   -- Each element must be a finished message (Data). A non-Data element, or an
   -- empty one, fails the whole bundle quietly (the shim marks the builder bad, so
   -- finish returns empty and oscLastError says why) instead of throwing into script.
   repeat for each element tElem in pMessages
      if tElem is a data then
         put tElem into tMsg
      else
         put the empty data into tMsg
      end if
      unsafe
         if the number of bytes in tMsg > 0 then
            _bundle_add_message(tH, dataPtr(tMsg), the number of bytes in tMsg)
         else
            _bundle_add_message(tH, nothing, 0)
         end if
      end unsafe
   end repeat
   put finishToData(tH, "bundle") into tResult
   unsafe
      _bundle_free(tH)
   end unsafe
   return tResult
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Parsing
-- =====================================================================
-- Read one parsed argument (by index) into its natural script value.
private handler readArg(in pH as Integer, in pIndex as Integer) returns any
   variable tType as Integer
   variable tOk as Integer
   variable tNum as Number
   variable tStr as String
   unsafe
      put _arg_type(pH, pIndex) into tType
   end unsafe
   if tType is 105 then        -- 'i'
      unsafe
         put _arg_int32(pH, pIndex, tOk) into tNum
      end unsafe
      return tNum
   else if tType is 102 then   -- 'f'
      unsafe
         put _arg_float(pH, pIndex, tOk) into tNum
      end unsafe
      return tNum
   else if tType is 100 then   -- 'd'
      unsafe
         put _arg_double(pH, pIndex, tOk) into tNum
      end unsafe
      return tNum
   else if tType is 115 then   -- 's'
      return shimText(kTxtArgString, pH, pIndex)
   else if tType is 104 then   -- 'h' int64
      return shimText(kTxtArgInt64, pH, pIndex)
   else if tType is 116 then   -- 't' timetag (unsigned NTP-64, also a decimal string)
      return shimText(kTxtArgInt64, pH, pIndex)
   else if tType is 98 then    -- 'b' blob -> Data
      return readBlob(pH, pIndex)
   else if tType is 84 then    -- 'T'
      return true
   else if tType is 70 then    -- 'F'
      return false
   end if
   return the empty string     -- 'N' nil / 'I' impulse carry no value
end handler

private handler readBlob(in pH as Integer, in pIndex as Integer) returns Data
   variable tNeed as Integer
   variable tCap as Integer
   variable tGot as Integer
   unsafe
      put _arg_blob(pH, pIndex, nothing, 0) into tNeed
   end unsafe
   if tNeed >= 0 then
      return the empty data       -- 0-length blob (or not a blob)
   end if
   put -tNeed into tCap
   ensureScratch(tCap)
   unsafe
      put _arg_blob(pH, pIndex, sScratchPtr, tCap) into tGot
   end unsafe
   return scratchToData(tGot)
end handler

-- Build the script-facing Array for one parsed message handle.
private handler messageArray(in pH as Integer) returns Array
   variable tArr as Array
   variable tAddr as String
   variable tTags as String
   variable tCount as Integer
   variable tArgs as List
   variable tI as Integer
   put shimText(kTxtAddress, pH, 0) into tAddr
   put shimText(kTxtTypetag, pH, 0) into tTags
   put false into tArr["isBundle"]
   put tAddr into tArr["address"]
   put tTags into tArr["types"]
   unsafe
      put _arg_count(pH) into tCount
   end unsafe
   put [] into tArgs
   if tCount > 0 then
      repeat with tI from 0 up to tCount - 1
         push readArg(pH, tI) onto tArgs
      end repeat
   end if
   put tArgs into tArr["args"]
   return tArr
end handler

-- oscParse(pData) -> Array
--   Message: ["isBundle":false, "address":"/x", "types":"fsi", "args":[...]]
--   Bundle:  ["isBundle":true, "timetag":"...", "messages":[array, array, ...]]
--   On malformed input: returns ["isBundle":false, "address":"", "error": ...].
/**
Summary: Reads an OSC message or bundle that arrived as bytes.

pData: The bytes of one datagram.

Returns: An array. For a message it holds "isBundle" (false), "address",
"types" (the type codes, such as "fsi") and "args" (the values, 1 to N).
For a bundle it holds "isBundle" (true), "timetag" (decimal text) and
"messages" (an array of message arrays, nested bundles included). For bytes
that are not OSC it holds "isBundle" (false), an empty "address", and
"error".

Example:
put oscParse(pData) into tMsg
if tMsg["address"] is "/1/fader1" then put tMsg["args"][1] into tLevel

Description: Integers and floats come back as numbers, h and t values as
decimal text (a script number cannot hold every 64-bit value), b values as
binary data, T and F as true and false, N and I as empty. A malformed
datagram never reads past its end; it is refused with a reason.
*/
public handler oscParse(in pData as Data) returns Array
   variable tH as Integer
   variable tErr as Array
   variable tResult as Array
   variable tIsBundle as Integer
   checkABI()
   put the empty string into sBindingError
   unsafe
      if the number of bytes in pData > 0 then
         put _osc_parse(dataPtr(pData), the number of bytes in pData) into tH
      else
         put _osc_parse(nothing, 0) into tH       -- the shim reports "datagram too short"
      end if
   end unsafe
   if tH is 0 then
      put false into tErr["isBundle"]
      put the empty string into tErr["address"]
      put oscLastError() into tErr["error"]
      return tErr
   end if
   unsafe
      put _is_bundle(tH) into tIsBundle
   end unsafe
   if tIsBundle is 1 then
      put buildBundleArray(tH) into tResult
   else
      put messageArray(tH) into tResult
   end if
   unsafe
      _parse_free(tH)        -- frees sub-messages too
   end unsafe
   return tResult
end handler

private handler buildBundleArray(in pH as Integer) returns Array
   variable tArr as Array
   variable tTag as String
   variable tCount as Integer
   variable tMessages as List
   variable tI as Integer
   variable tSub as Integer
   variable tSubIsBundle as Integer
   put true into tArr["isBundle"]
   put shimText(kTxtTimetag, pH, 0) into tTag
   put tTag into tArr["timetag"]
   unsafe
      put _bundle_count(pH) into tCount
   end unsafe
   put [] into tMessages
   if tCount > 0 then
      repeat with tI from 0 up to tCount - 1
         unsafe
            put _bundle_message(pH, tI) into tSub
         end unsafe
         -- A bundle element may itself be a bundle (OSC allows nesting; the C
         -- parser already validated it, depth-capped). Recurse so a nested bundle
         -- keeps its structure instead of collapsing to an empty message.
         unsafe
            put _is_bundle(tSub) into tSubIsBundle
         end unsafe
         if tSubIsBundle is 1 then
            push buildBundleArray(tSub) onto tMessages
         else
            push messageArray(tSub) onto tMessages
         end if
      end repeat
   end if
   put tMessages into tArr["messages"]
   return tArr
end handler

-- =====================================================================
-- Matching addresses, errors, and loading the library by hand
-- =====================================================================
-- oscMatch(pPattern, pAddress) -> Boolean   (OSC 1.0 wildcards ? * [] {})
--   A NUL in either string matches nothing (it cannot cross as a C string).
/**
Summary: Tells whether an OSC address matches an OSC address pattern.

pPattern: The pattern, which may use the OSC 1.0 wildcards: ? (one
character), * (any run of characters), [abc] and [a-z] (one of a set),
[!abc] (one not in a set) and {foo,bar} (one of the words). None of them
crosses a "/".

pAddress: The address to test, such as "/1/fader1".

Returns: true or false.

Example:
if oscMatch("/1/fader*", tMsg["address"]) then put tMsg["args"][1] into tLevel

Description: A pattern so complex it would take too long or nest too
deeply returns false rather than stalling the engine.
*/
public handler oscMatch(in pPattern as String, in pAddress as String) returns Boolean
   variable tR as Integer
   if hasNul(pPattern) or hasNul(pAddress) then
      return false
   end if
   unsafe
      put _osc_match(pPattern, pAddress) into tR
   end unsafe
   return tR is 1
end handler

-- oscLastError() -> String: why the last oscBuildMessage, oscBuildBundle or
--   oscParse refused (empty after one that succeeded).
/**
Summary: Says why the last build or parse was refused.

Returns: The reason, or empty when the last <oscBuildMessage>,
<oscBuildBundle> or <oscParse> succeeded.

Example:
put oscBuildMessage(tAddress, tArgs) into tData
if tData is empty then answer oscLastError()
*/
public handler oscLastError() returns String
   if sBindingError is not the empty string then
      return sBindingError
   end if
   return shimText(kTxtError, 0, 0)
end handler

-- Linux helper: preload oscxt.so by absolute path (no /usr/lib, no sudo, no
-- LD_LIBRARY_PATH). Returns 1 on success, 0 on failure (then oscLoadNativeLibError).
-- Flags 258 = RTLD_NOW | RTLD_GLOBAL. Gate the call to Linux when wiring it in.
/**
Summary: Loads the native library from a path you choose (Linux).

pPath: The full path of oscxt.so.

Returns: 1 when it loaded, 0 when it did not (<oscLoadNativeLibError> says
why).

Example:
if the platform is "Linux" then get oscLoadNativeLib(tFolder & "/oscxt.so")

Description: Only needed when the library is not where the engine looks for
it. Call it on Linux only, before any other call of this library.
*/
public handler oscLoadNativeLib(in pPath as String) returns Integer
   variable tHandle as optional Pointer
   put the empty string into sLoadError
   if hasNul(pPath) then
      put "oscxt: the path contains a NUL character" into sLoadError
      return 0
   end if
   bindLoaderErrorPath()
   unsafe
      put _dl_open(pPath, 258) into tHandle
   end unsafe
   if tHandle is nothing then
      put loaderErrorNow() into sLoadError
      return 0
   end if
   return 1
end handler

/**
Summary: Says why <oscLoadNativeLib> could not load the library.

Returns: The system's reason for the last <oscLoadNativeLib> that returned
0, or empty when the last one loaded the library (or none has run).

Description: The reason is read when the load fails and kept until the next
<oscLoadNativeLib>, so it can be asked for at any time after.
*/
public handler oscLoadNativeLibError() returns String
   return sLoadError
end handler

-- The engine binds a foreign handler on its FIRST call, through dlsym, and
-- that call consumes or frees the error text of a dlopen that just failed
-- (engine note 6.19). So the two handlers the error path needs are called
-- once BEFORE the dlopen whose error they read: the string copy (on a
-- one-byte empty C string) and dlerror itself, which also clears an older
-- error first, the POSIX idiom.
private handler bindLoaderErrorPath()
   variable tNulByte as Data
   variable tNulPtr as Pointer
   variable tEmpty as String
   variable tOk as Boolean
   variable tOlder as optional Pointer
   put the byte with code 0 into tNulByte
   unsafe
      put MCDataGetBytePtr(tNulByte) into tNulPtr
      put MCStringCreateWithCString(tNulPtr, tEmpty) into tOk
      put _dl_error() into tOlder
   end unsafe
end handler

-- dlerror()'s text, COPIED at once: it is libc's, so it comes back as a raw
-- Pointer and never as a ZString the engine would free (engine note 6.18).
private handler loaderErrorNow() returns String
   variable tPtr as optional Pointer
   variable tErr as String
   variable tOk as Boolean
   unsafe
      put _dl_error() into tPtr
   end unsafe
   if tPtr is nothing then
      return "oscxt: the library did not load, and the loader gave no reason"
   end if
   unsafe
      put MCStringCreateWithCString(tPtr, tErr) into tOk
   end unsafe
   if not tOk then
      return "oscxt: the library did not load (its reason could not be read)"
   end if
   return tErr
end handler

end library
